'''Amphithoë''' (am-fith′ọ̄-ē), ''n.'' [NL., < Gr. ἀμφί, on both sides, + θοός (fem. θοή), active, quick, < θέειν, run.] A genus of amphipodous edriophthalmous crustaceans, of the family ''Corophiidæ.'' {{smaller|The body is compressed and curved, and is composed of 15 distinct segments or somites, the head, formed of 7 anterior coalesced segments, counting as one.}}
[[FIGURE]]
{{c|{{smaller|''Amphithoë.''<br>''II′–XIV'', the appendage of the second-fourteenth somite; ''r'', rostrum; ''t'', telson; ''os'', oöstegite.}}}}
{{smaller|There are 7 free thoracic segments, each with a pair of appendages, 6 abdominal segments, and a small telson. The appendages of the first 3 abdominal segments are many-jointed bristly filaments, while the 3 posterior are styliform and serve as props when the animal leaps. The name is sometimes written, incorrectly, ''Ampithoë'' or ''Amphitoë.'' See cut under ''Amphipoda.''}}

'''amphithura''' (am-fi-thū′rä̤), ''n.'' Same as ''amphithyra.''

'''amphithyra''' (am-fith′i-rä̤), ''n.'' [ML., < LGr. ἀμφίθυρα, pl., ἀμφίθυρον, sing., neut. of Gr. ἀμφίθυρος, with a door on both sides, in LGr. being on both sides of the door, < ἀμφί, on both sides, + θύρα = E. ''door.''] In the ''Gr. Ch.'', a veil or curtain within the iconostasis. {{smaller|When drawn across it closes the opening left by the dwarf folding doors of the iconostasis, and entirely hides the altar and the celebrant from the view of any one not in the sanctuary. Several times during the service the curtain is drawn back to}}

