readings.c3s3:
{{smaller block|then in office. Amphoræ with painted decoration, having lids, and provided with bases enabling them to stand independently, served commonly as ornaments among the Greeks, and were given as prizes at some public games, much as cups are now given as prizes in racing and athletic sports. The Panathenaic amphoræ were large vases of this class, bearing designs relating to the worship of Athena, and, filled with oil from the sacred olives, were given at Athens as prizes to the victors in the Panathenaic games.}}
'''2.''' A liquid measure of the Greeks and Romans.
{{smaller block|The Greek amphora was probably equal to 24¼ liters, and the Roman amphora to 25½ liters in earlier and to 26 liters in later times.}}
'''3.''' In ''bot.'', the permanent basal portion of a pyxidium.—'''4.''' [''cap.''] [NL.] In ''zoöl.'': (''a'') A genus of ''Polygastrica''. ''Ehrenberg.'' (''b'') A genus of coleopterous insects. ''Wollaston.''—'''Bacchic amphora.''' {{smaller|See ''Bacchic''.}}

'''amphoral''' (am′fọ̄-ra̤l), ''a.'' [< L. ''amphoralis'', < ''amphora''.] Of, pertaining to, or resembling an amphora.

'''amphore†''', ''n.'' See ''amphor''.

'''amphoric''' (am-for′ik), ''a.'' [< ''amphora'' + ''-ic''.] Resembling the sound made by blowing across the mouth of a flask: applied to certain sounds obtained in auscultation and percussion of the chest: as, ''amphoric'' respiration; ''amphoric'' resonance; an ''amphoric'' voice, whisper, or cough.
{{smaller block|All the sounds called amphoric have a more or less musical quality, and usually indicate a cavity filled with air.}}

'''amphoricity''' (am-fọ̄-ris′i̤-ti), ''n.'' [< ''amphoric'' + ''-ity''.] The quality or condition of being amphoric.

'''amphorophony''' (am-fọ̄-rof′ọ̄-ni), ''n.'' [< L. ''amphora'', Gr. ἀμφορεύς, a jar, + φωνή, voice, sound.] Amphoric vocal resonance; an abnormal sound of the voice, noticed in auscultation
